<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Testament</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as OT</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Old Testament is the first division of the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, based primarily upon the 24 books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, or Tanakh, a collection of religious writings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and occasionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>. It differs from the Jewish canon in order, textual tradition and, in some traditions, contents: the Catholic canon has 46 books, the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a> up to 49 and the Protestant Bible typically 39. Christians traditionally divide it into the Pentateuch, the Historical books, the Poetic Books and the Prophetic books. Books in the Christian Old Testament but not in the Hebrew canon, mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, are called deuterocanonical.</p></div>
